/*
    This inline function was designed to get the table info and bias value for
   current input xx! lower:      indicate the lower boundary of the first table;
    upper:      indicate the upper boundary of the first table as well as the
   lower boundary of the second table; max:        indicate the upper boundary
   of the second table; stride0:    indicate the stride of the first table;
    stride1:    indicate the stride of the second table;
    xx:         indicate the inputs value;
    table_idx:  indicate the location of table info of input value xx;

    Points outside the stored table range are evaluated with the polynomial
   of the nearest boundary interval: below lower the first interval is
   extended to negative local coordinates, and beyond max the last interval
   is extended past its stored width. This keeps the tabulated value and its
   sensitivity one coherent response across the limits of the compressed
   table, instead of clipping to a constant (which would break the
   energy-force relationship) or inventing a new interval unsupported by the
   table samples.
*/

input.new_string:
/*
    The layout of a compressed table is defined by integer segment counts:
   the first sub-table spans [lower, upper) with stride0 and the second spans
   [upper, max) with stride1. Valid tables satisfy span == n * stride exactly,
   so the quotient must be rounded to the nearest integer instead of being
   truncated; otherwise floating-point representation error (e.g. 1.2 / 0.4
   evaluating to 2.9999999999999996) would silently shift the layout by one
   segment and break the value/sensitivity response at the sub-table seam.
*/
template <typename FPTYPE>
inline int table_seg_count(const FPTYPE& span, const FPTYPE& stride) {
  return (int)(span / stride + (FPTYPE)0.5);
}

